      "dom_id": "question-77230",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14123219/assets/8cf7d0520b1d0654d5749651595acd48b7a08f2b2d55142b18ca63b2ded20f55.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b$, find $\\lambda .$",
      "worked_solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14123219/assets/9edea1e3ad727d11d7bfac884f27c1b435233fa7df561dbe2a4f3af45ea16bfa.png)\n\nIn $△OBC$, we have:\n\n- $BC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=3a.$ \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =2b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=2b+\\lambda a=2b+3a.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$",
